=== where each design wins  ->  RESULTS.md section 2 prose ===================
[1] internal fragmentation (block_eff), trace=dl_transformer
      buddy 64.0%   freelist 64.0%   -> FREELIST wins by 0.0 pts
      predicted: buddy 55-70%, freelist 85-95% on DL shapes; roughly TIED
      on multi-MiB tensors because small_large_split puts freelist into a
      power-of-two ladder above 1 MiB -- see freelist_allocator.hpp banner.
[2] largest contiguous free block after full drain
      buddy 32.00 MiB   freelist 16.00 MiB   -> BUDDY wins (coalescing)
[3] allocate p99
      buddy 1038 ns   freelist 91 ns   -> FREELIST
      two competing costs, and which dominates depends on the trace:
      freelist pays a hash-map insert (live_) on every allocate; buddy pays
      a SPLIT CASCADE whenever a request needs a block far smaller than the
      nearest free ancestor (e.g. a small tensor after a run of large ones).
      On this DL trace's largest tensors (multi-MiB, close to the slab size)
      the split cascade dominates and buddy's p99 is the worse one -- the
      opposite of the uniform_pow2 trace, where blocks are smaller relative
      to the slab and buddy's simpler bookkeeping wins. Neither allocator is
      categorically faster; it is a function of block size vs. slab depth.
[4] deallocate p99
      buddy 597 ns   freelist 610 ns   -> BUDDY

  *** [2] and [4] are the SAME mechanism (coalescing) showing up as a win
  *** in one row and a cost in the other. If you remember one result from
  *** Phase 2, remember this pair.


